            { type: `written`, q: `a) Consider the following continuous-time signal:<br><b>f(t) = A cos(1000πt) − B sin(50πt) + C cos(200πt) − D sin(250πt)</b><br>where A and B are fixed and greater than 0. Determine the lowest possible <b>sampling interval</b> to sample the signal without aliasing effects. Also, compute the <b>phase of the frequency components</b>.`, ans: `<b>Frequencies</b> (f = coefficient of πt ÷ 2): 1000πt → <b>500 Hz</b>; 50πt → <b>25 Hz</b>; 200πt → <b>100 Hz</b>; 250πt → <b>125 Hz</b>.<br>fmax = 500 Hz (A &gt; 0) → fs ≥ 2 × 500 = <b>1000 Hz</b> → T = 1/1000 = <b>0.001 s (1 ms)</b>.<br><b>Phases</b>: no constant is added inside any bracket, so read directly every component has phase <b>0</b> (500 Hz: 0, 25 Hz: 0, 100 Hz: 0, 125 Hz: 0).<br>Written in the L2 form a·sin(ωt + φ) with positive amplitudes: A cos(1000πt) = A sin(1000πt + π/2) → <b>π/2 (90°)</b>; −B sin(50πt) = B sin(50πt + π) → <b>π (180°)</b>; C cos(200πt) → <b>π/2 (90°)</b>; −D sin(250πt) → <b>π (180°)</b>.`, why: `L4 method: f = ω/2π, fs = 2·fmax, T = 1/fs. L2: z(t) = a·sin(ωt + φ), and a cosine is a sine 90° out of phase; a minus sign is a further 180°. The paper does not say which form it wants, so both readings are shown — say which one you use.` },
            { type: `written`, q: `b) An audio signal is sampled such that the sampling interval equal to 500 milliseconds. If the sample size is 64 bits and duration is 5.5 hours. What is the file size for this stereo Audio signal?`, ans: `fs = 1/0.5 s = <b>2 Hz</b>; t = 5.5 × 3600 = <b>19,800 s</b>; stereo = 2.<br>Size = 19,800 × 2 × 64 × 2 = <b>5,068,800 bits</b> = 633,600 B = <b>618.75 KB</b>.`, why: `Exactly the course summary's Ex 3 (L4 notes). Audio size = time × fs × bits × channels.` }
